# Chapter 6, second part: Preview and the Viewer.  Included by the chapter file.

.h2 Preview [print-preview]
.ix Preview
.ix Preview Document panel
.ix Preview Title Page?
.ix Preview from Section
.ix F12, Print, Preview
.ix Document, Block, Section, Preview
.ix Preview, wait panel
Preview shows the pages of a part of the document on the screen, in the Viewer.
Nothing that you keep is written.

.h3 How to reach it
Preview is the third entry of the Print menu and opens a menu of three
entries.  {k:F12}, {k:P}, {k:P}, then {k:D}, {k:B} or {k:S}.
.table 10 8 32
Entry | Letter | The part that is previewed
Document | {k:D} | The whole document, or a range of sections: a panel prompts first (below).
Block | {k:B} | The block the cursor is in.  If the cursor is in no block, the
 box "The cursor is not in a block" (title "Preview") is shown.
Section | {k:S} | The section the cursor is in, with all that is below it.  If
 there is none, the box "There is no current section" is shown.
.end
.pic pictures/print-preview-menu.png 5.4 The Preview menu.

.h3 The Preview Document panel
Only Document puts questions.  The panel is titled {b:Preview Document}.
.table 18 12 20
Field | Values | Meaning
Preview Title Page? | Yes (default), No | Yes puts the blocks before the first
 level 1 section (the title page) first.  No leaves them out.  Not used when a
 range is given.
Preview from Section | A section number, or empty | The first section to show.
 Empty means the first section.
to Section | A section number, or empty | The last section to show, with its
 subsections.  Empty means the end.
Error Log? | Yes (default), No | Yes ends the pages with the Error Log when the
 document has errors or warnings ({n:print-errlog}).  The faults are
 marked in the text either way, and the banner and the marks are in the
 pages either way ({n:print-failed}).
.end
The checks and the messages are those of Final ({n:print-final}): "Only
a structured document has sections", "Cannot find section number NUMBER" and
"The first section is after the last".
.pic pictures/print-preview-doc-panel.png 5.4 The Preview Document panel.

.h3 What happens
.ix Starting viewer
.ix Preview done
.ix Preview cancelled
1. Checks.  The Printer and the Viewer must be found, and the Viewer needs an
 X display.  If not, nothing is started and a box titled "Preview" shows what
 is missing (the table below).
2. Preparing.  The part is taken as it is in the Editor, with the changes
 that are not saved.  The file of the document is not touched and the
 document is not marked as saved.
3. Typesetting.  The wait panel shows the stage and the pages
 ({n:print-progress}); {k:Esc} cancels.
4. The Viewer.  When the pages are made the panel shows "Starting viewer" and
 "Close the viewer to return.", and the Viewer opens on them.  The Editor
 waits until the Viewer ends.  In the X11 version of the Editor the Viewer
 is a window of its own and the Editor goes on serving its window.  In the
 terminal version the Editor gives the terminal back (as for a shell) while
 the Viewer runs and takes it again afterwards; keys typed in the meantime
 are dropped.
5. Whatever the end is, nothing of the Preview is left on the disk.

Close the Viewer ({k:Q} or {k:Esc} in it) to return to the Editor.

.h3 The wait panel
The panel is titled {b:Preview} and is the one of {x:print-progress}: the stage ("Writing", "Running LaTeX (pass N)",
"Starting viewer"), "Page N of about M", a gauge and the hint "Esc cancels".
The number of pages is an estimate at first, made from the page size, the
margins, the spacing and the page breaks of the document, the room of its
tables and pictures, and the pages of the contents, the lists and the index
that the settings of the document make.  When the document has conditional
text, the estimate counts the branches that this build prints, as the print
does.  It is usually within 10 percent
before the first page is made and within a few percent when half the pages
are done, and it is exact when the last page is made.

.h3 Messages
.table 31 29
Message | Meaning
Preview cancelled | {k:Esc} in the wait panel.
Preview done | The Viewer ended, and the run told nothing about the errors of
 the
 document (see the next row).
Preview done: PRINT FAILED, N print errors (Ctrl-F8 finds the next) | The
 Viewer ended and the document has errors; markers were put in the text
 ({n:print-errmarks}).  With warnings only the words "PRINT FAILED, " are not
 there, and "Preview done: no print errors" says that there are none.
LaTeX failed: ! TEXT l.12 THE LINE | LaTeX stopped and made no pages.  The
 first error line of the TeX log and the line after it.
PRINT FAILED: Preview shown in spite of errors of LaTeX: ! TEXT l.12 THE
 LINE | LaTeX found
 an error but still made pages, which are shown.  The message comes when the
 Viewer ends.
Preview done; the viewer could not show N things: TEXT | The Viewer left
 something out of the pages (TEXT names the first); the preview is otherwise
 good.  The same words can follow "Preview done: no print errors".
Preview: the viewer failed: TEXT | The Viewer ended with an error; TEXT is the
 first reason that it gave.  When the run has a log, either line ends with
 "; details in the log of the run" ({n:appcfg-log}).
Preview failed: ... TEXT | Any other message of the Printer.
Preview failed (... ended with status N) | The Printer stopped and gave no
 reason.
Preview needs ...: it was not found next to ... or in the system directories. |
 (box) The Printer is missing.
Preview needs the viewer ...: it was not found next to ... or in the system
 directories. | (box) The Viewer is missing.
Preview needs an X display: {c:DISPLAY} is not set.  Run ... under X, or use the
 X11 version. | (box) There is no display for the Viewer.
Preview: cannot start ...: REASON | The Printer could not be run.
Preview: cannot run the viewer FILE | The Viewer could not be run.
.end
.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
.note For developers: {c:STRATA_DVIVIEW} names the Viewer in place of the one next
  to the Editor or in the system directories.  When it is set, the check of {c:DISPLAY} is skipped.
.endif

.h3 Edges
.table 22 38
Case | What happens
Document with Include markers | The parts that the markers name are read from
 their files, as in a Print ({n:marker-include-print}).
Part of a document (Block, Section, a range) | Its sections have the numbers
 that they have on the screen, and its pages start at 1.
Document with unsaved changes | The text in the Editor is previewed, not the
 file.
Long documents | The wait panel keeps up; {k:Esc} stops at once.
.end

.h2 The Viewer [print-dviview]
.ix Viewer
.ix DVI viewer
.ix Viewer, status bar
.ix Viewer, keys
.ix Go to page, Viewer
.ix Zoom, Viewer
The Viewer shows the pages of a Preview in a window of its own, at the exact
size of the page.  Preview starts it.  The Viewer
needs an X display: without one, Preview reports this and does not start.  The
Viewer is for reading the layout; the PDF of Final is the exact page, for the
printer.

.pic pictures/print-viewer-first.png 5.4 The Viewer and its status bar.

.h3 The window
The window opens at the size of the first page at 100 percent zoom (a US
letter page is 850 by 1100 pixels), with the status bar added under the page,
and never larger than the screen.  The window can be resized; the page keeps
its place at the top left and the part that does not show is reached with
the cursor keys.  A landscape page is shown sideways.  Everything in the
Viewer is done with keys.

.h3 The status bar
.ix Status bar, Viewer
The bar is a grey strip under the page, of one line or two (three with the red
area of {n:print-viewer-status}).
.table 12 48
Line | What it shows
Red area | Only for a failed print: the first line of the bar, red, with
 "PRINT FAILED - N errors - THIS PREVIEW IS INACCURATE"
 ({n:print-viewer-status}).
First | "Page N of M   Zoom Z%   F1 or ? hides keys" (the last words are
 "shows keys" while the legend is hidden).  N is the page now shown, M the
 number of pages.
Second | The key legend, one line.  It is shown when the Viewer starts and is
 hidden and shown again with {k:F1} or {k:?}.  With it hidden the bar has one
 line and the page has the room.
Go to page | While a page number is being typed, the first line is replaced by
 "Go to page (1-M): NN_   Enter goes, Esc cancels".
.end
The legend has four texts, and the longest one that fits the width of the
window is shown, so that a narrow window shows less.
.table 40 11
Legend | Window width
{c:n PgDn Space: next   p PgUp: previous   Home End: first last   g: go to page
 + -: zoom   0: 100%   arrows: pan   q: quit} | Wide
{c:n p: page   g: go to page   + - 0: zoom   arrows: pan   q: quit} | Medium
{c:n p: page   g: go to   + - 0: zoom   q: quit} | Narrow
{c:g: go to   q: quit} | Narrowest
.end

.h3 The red status area of a failed print [print-viewer-status]
.ix Viewer, red status area
.ix Red status area
.ix Failed Preview, Viewer
When the print had errors the pages carry a banner and marks ({n:print-failed})
and the Viewer adds a status of its own, so that it cannot be missed even when
the page is zoomed or scrolled so that the banner is out of sight.  The bar
then has a first line that is {b:red} (dark red, with white bold letters):
.code
PRINT FAILED - 3 errors - THIS PREVIEW IS INACCURATE
.end
("1 error" in the singular).  The area stays on every page and at every zoom,
the key legend and the entry of a page number come below it, and the window
is one line higher to hold it.  A good print, or one made without the banner, has no red
area, and the window is the same as before.  The Viewer only shows the status
and makes no marks, and
the marks on the pages are those of the Printer.
.pic pictures/print-viewer-failed.png 4.2 The Viewer on page 2 of a failed Preview: the banner at the top of the page, and the red status area above the status bar.

.h3 The keys
.table 24 36
Key | What it does
{k:N}, {k:Space}, {k:PgDn} | The next page.  On the last page nothing happens.
{k:P}, {k:PgUp} | The previous page.  On the first page nothing happens.
{k:Home}, {k:End} | The first page, the last page.
{k:G} | Go to a page: the bar prompts for a number (below).
{k:Up}, {k:Down}, {k:Left}, {k:Right} | Pan the page by 60 pixels.  The page
 cannot be moved beyond its edges.
{k:+}, {k:=}, {k:Keypad +} | Zoom in by 25 percent, up to 800 percent.
{k:-}, {k:Keypad -} | Zoom out by 25 percent, down to 25 percent.
{k:0}, {k:Keypad 0}, {k:Keypad *} | Back to 100 percent.
{k:F1}, {k:?} | Show or hide the key legend (the second line of the bar).
{k:Q}, {k:Esc} | Close the Viewer.  The window manager's close also does.
.end
A new page starts at the top left.  The zoom keeps the center of the window
where it was.  The zoom is not a window size: the window keeps its size.

.h3 Go to a page
{k:G} (or {k:G} with Shift) opens an entry in the status bar.  The line shows
"Go to page (1-M): " and the digits typed so far.  Digits are accepted, to a
maximum of eight; {k:Backspace} deletes the last; {k:Enter} goes to the page,
a number past the last page going to the last page and 0 to the first;
{k:Esc} leaves the entry and the page stays.  While the entry is open no
other key acts: {k:Q} does not close the Viewer, and {k:Esc} closes the
entry only.  {k:Enter} with no digits changes nothing.

.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
.h3 Options and environment
The Viewer is started by Preview with the name of the DVI file only.  Its
options are these.
.table 13 47
Option | Meaning
{c:FILE.dvi} | The DVI file.  Required: the first word that does not begin with a
 minus.
{c:-p PAGE} | Start at page PAGE (1 is the first).  A number out of range is
 brought into range; a word that is not a number gives page 1.
{c:-d DPI} | The resolution of 100 percent zoom, 10 to 2000; the default is 100.
 A value out of range is a mistake of the command line.
{c:--report FILE} | Write one line, "fail: TEXT" or "note: TEXT", for each failure
 and each fault of the run to FILE.  The Editor uses it to learn what went
 wrong.
{c:--var NAME VALUE}, {c:--special NAME VALUE} | Accepted and ignored: the Viewer
 shows pages that are made already.  The options let every program that the
 Document Manager starts take the same ones.
{c:-- FILE.dvi} | The next word is the file, whatever it begins with.
.end
A second file name, an unknown option and an option with no value make the
Viewer write a usage line on the standard error and end with status 2.  The
Viewer ends with status 1 when the DVI file cannot be read or the display
cannot be
opened.  {x:appcmd-dviview} has the same table with the keys and the exit
status.
.table 17 43
Environment variable | Meaning
{c:DVIVIEW_PLAIN} | When set: no status bar; the title of the window is "NAME -
 page N/M - Z%".
{c:DVIVIEW_TIMING} | When set: the log of the Viewer holds the time that the Viewer takes to draw each page (the log of the developer edition with detail logging).
.end
.endif
